! Floating-point diagnostic jb_af1c, routine F210 "STORE ROUNDED TEST
! ROUTINE", section B "TEST UNCHANGED REGISTERS", sub-tests B1-B2
! (docs/jb_af1c_floating-point.pdf p.111, octal 016064.00-016107.40,
! the routine's own exit "BD,$+1.0").
!
! The oracle for one sentence of the manual (p.96): "The entire
! accumulator and its sign byte register remain unchanged throughout
! the operation unless used as the effective address." Nothing in
! F210A checks it -- every sub-test there reads ROS, never the
! accumulator -- and the model satisfies it only by not writing them,
! which is the kind of thing that stays true by accident until someone
! adds a write.
!
! Same shape as F209C, applied to SRD instead of D+MG. Each sub-test
! sets all three registers to a known pattern, runs one SRD(U), and
! then asks three separate questions:
!
!   B1  C0000 zeroes the accumulator left and right and CM0000 the
!       sign byte; after SRD(U) ROS, CT0101 checks L0-63 and R0-63 are
!       still zero and CT0011 checks SB0-7.
!   B2  C1111/CM1111 set all three to ones; CT1010, CT1010 and CT1100
!       check they are still ones.
!
! B2 is the stronger of the two, for the same reason F209C2 was: an
! all-ones accumulator has EF = 1, so the flagged path runs, and an
! all-ones sign byte means the sign and all three flag bits are set --
! so a model that wrote back any part of what it computed would be
! caught. B1's zeros would survive several kinds of wrong write.
!
! Note that ROS itself is never checked here. These six questions are
! about what SRD did NOT touch, not about what it stored; F210A
! already covered the result.
!
! FSR2 is the listing's own label and sits at its real address,
! 016064.00 -- the routine's exit branches back to it.
!
! ROS (022355) is the routine's scratch word, not a pool constant.
! Framework substitutes as everywhere else in this series: SERS (octal
! 1304) -> FAIL halt, SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch. Constants from the
! pool p.176. PASS sits at 016107.40, on the routine's exit; the four
! rows after it (BD,$+1.0 / B,FSR2 / SIC,SENO+.32 / B,SSW) are the
! SSIP return and are not transcribed. F210C "ACCUMULATOR FLAGS TO
! MEMORY FLAGS" begins at 016111.40 with label FSR3.
!
! Instructions: C0000, CM0000, C1111, CM1111, SRD(U), CT0101, CT0011,
! CT1010, CT1100, SIC, B, BRGZ.
! verify_fp_arith_f210b_listing.py checks every word bit-for-bit.
!
! Observed: passed on the first run, and probing it turned up two
! things worth keeping.
!
! First, B2's questions are live. Redirecting SRD's one write to the
! accumulator's left half halts B2A at 016103.40, and redirecting it
! to the sign byte halts B2C at 016107.40 -- each the address right
! after its own BRGZ. B1's questions do NOT fire under either: its
! registers are zero and a zero write leaves them zero. That confirms
! the reading above, that B2 is the load-bearing half.
!
! Second, and more interesting: SRD CANNOT be made to violate the rule
! by ADDING a write. Putting an st.writeWord(addrAccSignByte, ...)
! alongside the existing st.writeWord(ea, ...) is a compile error, not
! a test failure -- bsc G0004, "Rule RL_rl_exec_resume uses methods
! that conflict in parallel: st_accSignByte.write(...) and
! st_accSignByte.write(...)" -- because st.writeWord(ea, ...) may
! itself target the sign byte, which is exactly the manual's "unless
! used as the effective address". A variable-address write and a
! fixed-address write to the same register cannot be scheduled
! together in one arm. So today the structure of the E-box enforces
! the rule that F210B checks, and the only way to break it is to
! redirect the write rather than add one.
!
! That is worth remembering rather than celebrating: the guarantee
! belongs to the current write-port arrangement, not to SRD. The
! planned E-box refactor -- routing storage through request/response
! methods (plan section 1c step 5c) -- changes exactly that
! arrangement, and F210B is what will notice if the rule is lost in
! the process. That is the case for keeping it in the suite even
! though no single-line mutation isolates it today.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes --
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- the routine's scratch word --
        SLC, (8)22355.0
ROS     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)022005.0
FPK0    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000000
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777

! -- code (verbatim, at the listing's addresses) --
        SLC, (8)16064.0
FSR2    C0000(BU,64), FPK0, 64                 ! F210B1: 0 s remain 0 s
        C0000(BU,64), FPK0
        CM0000(BU,8), 10.0
        SRD(U), ROS
        CT0101(BU,64), FPK0, 64                ! B1A: does L0-63 remain + 0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0101(BU,64), FPK0                    ! B1B: does R0-63 remain + 0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,8), 10.0                     ! B1C: does SB0-7 remain + 0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        B, $+.32                               ! F210B2: 1 s remain 1 s
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1, 64
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1
        CM1111(BU,8), 10.0
        SRD(U), ROS
        CT1010(BU,64), FPK1, 64                ! B2A: does L0-63 remain + 1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1010(BU,64), FPK1                    ! B2B: does R0-63 remain + 1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,8), 10.0                     ! B2C: does SB0-7 remain + 1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                                 ! 016107.40: the routine's exit ("BD,$+1.0")
        END, FSR2
